Original commit body from 9bff38c135ee30c7996489d460347c748630cdb9:

The import_edges requirement behind the partial-requirement warning
counted every non-stdlib Rust/Python import edge, including imports the
Python analyzer had resolved to the repo's own files. A pyproject.toml
package with no dependencies whose modules import each other warned
"found 2 third-party Rust/Python import edges but 0 TOML dependency
declarations".

Discriminator: an edge whose dst is an in-repo symbol id is first-party
(the ADR-0037 definition, and the same dst-in-symbol-ids test the
finalize boundary step uses). Edge.is_resolved cannot be read instead:
when linkers run it is still the producer's advisory default (True),
external imports included.

Rust analogue: Rust import dsts never resolve, so a crate's own modules
(`use util::helper` beside `mod util;`, `src/net/mod.rs` -> `net`) and
its own Cargo package name (`use my_crate::x` from tests/ or src/bin/)
are recognised by name and not counted.

The module docstring and requirement comment, reworded by the
2026-10-10 staleness audit to describe the bug, now describe the fixed
behaviour. Also re-points a stale dependency.py:49 comment citation in
test_io_boundary.py (now :69).

